{"name":"Breaker Bay","hex":"#517b78","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"breaker","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Something that breaks (something else).","A machine for breaking rocks, or for breaking coal at the mines."]},{"part":"Interjection","definitions":["Used to open a conversation or call for a response on CB radio."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/breaker"},{"term":"bay","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Batuley."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A body of water (especially the sea) contained by a concave shoreline.","A bank or dam to keep back water."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bay"}],"entries":[{"title":"Breaker Bay","blocks":[{"text":"Breaker Bay is a suburb on the south east coast of Wellington City in New Zealand, on the Miramar Peninsula. The suburb contains a 600 m wide bay with the same name."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7c/BreakerB3.jpg/330px-BreakerB3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Breaker_Bay","wikidata":"Q4959431"},{"title":"Bay","blocks":[{"text":"A bay is a recessed, coastal body of water that directly connects to a larger main body of water, such as an ocean, a lake, or another bay. A large bay is usually called a gulf, sea, sound, or bight. A cove is a small, circular bay with a narrow entrance. A fjord is an elongated bay formed by glacial action.\nThe term embayment is also used for related features, such as extinct bays or freshwater environments."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Aerial_photograph_of_Langness_and_the_bay_at_Castletown_13_February_2014_-_geograph.org.uk_-_3847162.jpg/330px-Aerial_photograph_of_Langness_and_the_bay_at_Castletown_13_February_2014_-_geograph.org.uk_-_3847162.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https